{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfihPQMUuzigtQsZhfYAp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written all 8 test statistics for critiquing the incumbent `person_specific_motif_suspicion` into `iter_2/critique/test_stats/`. I ran each one once on the observed data (80 participants × 64 trials). All return a finite number and each takes under 0.1 s, well inside the 0.3 s-per-call budget. The pipeline will run the posterior-predictive check, so there are no p-values yet.\n\n| Statistic | What it tests | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `participant_side_bias_spread` | How much people differ in their Left/Right bias. Kept from last round because it was the only significant result (p=0.014), and the new model still has no side bias. | 0.076 |\n| `response_side_perseveration` | How often a person picks the same side as on their previous trial. The model treats every trial as independent. | 0.513 |\n| `alternation_choice_person_spread` | How much people differ in picking a perfectly alternating sequence (HTHT…). This directly tests the change this model adds: each person's own suspicion of repeating patterns. | 0.401 |\n| `item_consensus_extremity` | How strongly people agree on each pair, measured as how far each pair's choice rate is from 50/50, averaged over pairs. Tests whether the model is too decisive or not decisive enough. | 0.262 |\n| `over_alternation_switch_preference` | When both sequences switch between H and T five or more times, how often people pick the one that switches more. Tests whether people penalise too much alternation more than the model does. | 0.368 |\n| `final_run_avoidance_equal_switches` | When both sequences switch equally often, how often people pick the one with the shorter streak at the end. Tests a position effect that switch count doesn't capture. | 0.500 |\n| `switch_preference_person_spread` | How much people differ in preferring sequences that switch more, leaving out perfect alternation. Tests whether the model's spread of switch beliefs is right. | 0.153 |\n| `heads_count_preference` | How often people pick the sequence with more heads. The model treats H and T identically. | 0.473 |\n\nEach file's `# description:` header says how to read the direction (observed above vs. below what the model predicts).\n\nOn the observed data, `final_run_avoidance_equal_switches` comes out at exactly 0.5. So people show no preference there, and that statistic will only count as a discrepancy if the model predicts a clear lean either way."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":388,"cache_read_input_tokens":52994,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":388,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":680,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"936aaeb2-2651-49a5-9669-8bc85b08b40b","uuid":"f4f400c4-87fc-40b3-bf40-ea93576744e4","timestamp":"2026-10-05T07:29:31.653Z","request_id":"req_011CfihPPxBCAm6ZxQXbU2Kc"}
